{"page":217,"blocks":[{"x":0.6653,"y":0.05257,"w":0.28621,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"3.11 Linearization and Differentials 203"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.09343,"w":0.59308,"h":0.07419,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"give good approximations to the y-values on the curve. We observe this phenomenon by zooming in on the two graphs at the point of tangency or by looking at tables of values for the difference between ƒ(x) and its tangent line near the x-coordinate of the point of tan- gency. The phenomenon is true not just for parabolas; every differentiable curve behaves locally like its tangent line."},{"x":0.21478,"y":0.09944,"w":0.04273,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = f(x)"},{"x":0.26298,"y":0.11539,"w":0.07182,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Slope = f′(a)"},{"x":0.13927,"y":0.14387,"w":0.1404,"h":0.01342,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"y = L(x) (a, f(a))"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.1695,"w":0.59303,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"In general, the tangent to y = ƒ(x) at a point x = a, where ƒ is differentiable (Figure 3.51), passes through the point (a, ƒ(a)), so its point-slope equation is"},{"x":0.09677,"y":0.20359,"w":0.08978,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 a"},{"x":0.57204,"y":0.20787,"w":0.17056,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = ƒ(a) + ƒ′(a)(x - a)."},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.23081,"w":0.21346,"h":0.02763,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 3.51 The tangent to the curve y = ƒ(x) at x = a is the line"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.23157,"w":0.36759,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Thus, this tangent line is the graph of the linear function"},{"x":0.56183,"y":0.25391,"w":0.19055,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"L(x) = ƒ(a) + ƒ′(a)(x - a)."},{"x":0.08867,"y":0.26166,"w":0.17157,"h":0.01212,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"L(x) = ƒ(a) + ƒ′(a)(x - a)."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.27761,"w":0.5888,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"For as long as this line remains close to the graph of ƒ as we move off the point of tan- gency, L(x) gives a good approximation to ƒ(x)."},{"x":0.39285,"y":0.33987,"w":0.51208,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"DEFINITIONS If ƒ is differentiable at x = a, then the approximating function"},{"x":0.56387,"y":0.36289,"w":0.18646,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"L(x) = ƒ(a) + ƒ′(a)(x - a)"},{"x":0.39285,"y":0.38657,"w":0.31823,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"is the linearization of ƒ at a. The approximation"},{"x":0.61613,"y":0.40893,"w":0.08183,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(x) ≈L(x)"},{"x":0.39285,"y":0.43195,"w":0.53249,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"of ƒ by L is the standard linear approximation of ƒ at a. The point x = a is the center of the approximation."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.49369,"w":0.54677,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 1 Find the linearization of ƒ(x) = 21 + x at x = 0 (Figure 3.52)."},{"x":0.90363,"y":0.53672,"w":0.00654,"h":0.02139,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2 x"},{"x":0.85469,"y":0.54129,"w":0.04397,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"y = 1 +"},{"x":0.48157,"y":0.54472,"w":0.16162,"h":0.03342,"fs":0.01023,"n":7,"k":0,"t":"5 4 x 4 1 x 2"},{"x":0.45519,"y":0.5501,"w":0.1767,"h":0.02222,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"y = + y = +"},{"x":0.71267,"y":0.55601,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"1.1"},{"x":0.86446,"y":0.59333,"w":0.0668,"h":0.01151,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"y = \"1 + x"},{"x":0.46027,"y":0.60462,"w":0.06693,"h":0.01151,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"y = \"1 + x"},{"x":0.71267,"y":0.60942,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"1.0"},{"x":0.71267,"y":0.66129,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"0.9"},{"x":0.3356,"y":0.66443,"w":0.05964,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 −1"},{"x":0.44641,"y":0.66497,"w":0.15888,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"1 2 3 4"},{"x":0.71755,"y":0.66995,"w":0.2282,"h":0.01108,"fs":0.01023,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"0 −0.1 0.1 0.2"},{"x":0.31841,"y":0.69569,"w":0.34907,"h":0.04297,"fs":0.01151,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 3.52 The graph of y = 21 + x and its linear- izations at x = 0 and x = 3. Figure 3.53 shows a magni- fied view of the small window about 1 on the y-axis."},{"x":0.71267,"y":0.69612,"w":0.23799,"h":0.02762,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 3.53 Magnified view of the window in Figure 3.52."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.75748,"w":0.1118,"h":0.01365,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Solution Since"},{"x":0.64118,"y":0.77962,"w":0.0879,"h":0.02851,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"2(1 + x)-1>2,"},{"x":0.58502,"y":0.7811,"w":0.06434,"h":0.01983,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ′(x) = 1"},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.80839,"w":0.39416,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"we have ƒ(0) = 1 and ƒ′(0) = 1>2, giving the linearization"},{"x":0.71286,"y":0.84141,"w":0.13191,"h":0.02721,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2(x - 0) = 1 + x 2."},{"x":0.46943,"y":0.84159,"w":0.2516,"h":0.01982,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"L(x) = ƒ(a) + ƒ′(a)(x - a) = 1 + 1"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.87932,"w":0.10723,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"See Figure 3.53."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.89702,"w":0.59302,"h":0.03858,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The following table shows how accurate the approximation 21 + x ≈1 + (x>2) from Example 1 is for some values of x near 0. As we move away from zero, we lose"}]}